On Dec 7, 2007, Doug Freyburger wrote:
> Current bones file processing converts.  Maybe a better mechanism
> is to delete the object if it collides with an existing artifact or
> the character's quest artifact - Artifacts are supposed to be unique
> and held by the dieties and/or the quest nemesis so the one in the
> bones file just called back by the dieties or retrieved by the
> nemesis.

Why not put the cart before the horse? That is, have the game decide
in advance which bones file, if any, will be linked into the game at
the beginning. Then you can leave any artifacts from the bones file
where they are, and simply not generate those artifacts in the game.
Obviously, you'll still have to remove any crucial artifacts like the
bell, book, candelabrum, or the player's own quest artifact, if it
exists, but as Derek Ray pointed out elsewhere in the thread, you can
rationalize this as having these artifacts re-stolen by Rodney, the
quest Nemesis, et al.

I can think of a few problems with that approach, though. For one,
an observant player might be able to tell in advance that they are
going to receive a bones file by watching the size of their save.
Also, in cases where a class is guaranteed to receive a certain
artifact as their first gift, they may discover that they are bound
to encounter a bones file with that artifact. The same goes for
attempting to create artifacts by naming.

I think that any way you turn it, bones files are always going to be
a compromise.


